{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:56.121772Z","id":"1fcead51d80302234c7ea809a457b02f730ff49d624ecc77ea5d05f71250c985","body":{"detail":"Failure: board halted at time t (e.g. `humility` attached); the page reads board/writes/0/64 and gets now = write_stamp(t) = S; the probe then writes RAM via Machine::debug_write (crates/armv6m/src/lib.rs:671), which stamps the word S because board time is frozen; the page's next poll is board/writes/S/64 (web/memory.js:309, which sends `since = this.now`), and bus.rs:225 / bus.rs:246 test `last > since`, so S > S is false and the probe's write is never lit. The same loss happens while running: write_stamp truncates to microseconds (bus.rs:83-85), so stores later in the microsecond the response was taken in carry stamp S and are skipped until that word is written again. The heat map then shows a cell as cold when it was written.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":219},"end":{"line":250},"content_hash":"2b6e82e299750fddedae78fb7bb385581417d4f88753664e8ba7dd36f75cb98d"},"suggested_fix":"Compare with `last >= since` in both written_since and written_in. Re-sending the boundary microsecond's cells is harmless: the page overwrites each cell's stamp with the same value (web/memory.js:325).","summary":"written_since/written_in use `last > since`, so a write stamped in the same microsecond as the `now` a poll returned is never reported to the page","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:56.121832Z","id":"2338ac20970d673fa6e2aece284b71123d8efce100bb61bddd6cc5f72d29993e","body":{"detail":"The kernel (and with it this crate) is built for wasm32-unknown-unknown (README.md:40, crates/kernel/Cargo.toml:31), where usize is 32 bits. For GET board/writes/0/4/0x20000100/0xFFFFFFF0 (crates/kernel/src/server.rs:170), offset 0x100 + 0xFFFFFFF0 overflows: a debug build panics on the add, a release build wraps to 0xF0, so `to` = 0x3C < `from` = 0x40 and `ram.written[from..to]` at bus.rs:244 panics. A panic in the kernel traps the whole container from one malformed path. Related: server.rs:162-175 truncate each parsed u64 with `as u32`, so since=0x1_0000_0000 silently becomes 0 and base=0x1_2000_0000 becomes 0x2000_0000; and writes_in_json (crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1464-1474) echoes the unclamped `length` as the RAM's length, and names a non-RAM base as \"RAM\" with no cells, so the page cannot tell the read fell outside memory.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":236},"end":{"line":244},"content_hash":"87bd16259d0d0a024956518ce03dba5138167a77ffbb24a78f2c8ef90c73f533"},"suggested_fix":"In written_in compute the end in u64 (or `offset.saturating_add(length as usize)`) and clamp it to the RAM's end before div_ceil. In server.rs refuse numbers above u32::MAX with `u32::try_from(..).ok().ok_or(Refusal::NotFound)?`. In writes_in_json report the clamped length and refuse (or return null) when `base` is not in a RAM.","summary":"written_in adds the user-supplied length to the RAM offset in usize, which overflows on the wasm32 kernel and then panics slicing `written[from..to]`","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:56.121952Z","id":"1cfb2c5edc394fe6d926f8d263e00f80d131c41f544df60682defa7a6e4c50b5","body":{"detail":"write_stamp (bus.rs:83-85) clamps to u32::MAX, so after 2^32 microseconds (~71.6 min of simulated time; the busy-wait skip in crates/armv6m/src/lib.rs `spin` lets simulated time run well ahead of wall time) every write gets the stamp u32::MAX and `now` stays u32::MAX. The page then polls with since = u32::MAX (web/memory.js:309), `last > since` (bus.rs:225, 246) is never true, and no further write is ever shown, with no error. The written order between words is also lost from then on, so written_json's stamps stop meaning anything.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":66},"end":{"line":85},"content_hash":"cc487d35a0e8a9cbbeb72cae7d86d0430e3fadc1ac84c68e4551866601d9813c"},"suggested_fix":"Keep stamps as u64 (doubling Ram::written), or use a coarser unit sized for long runs (e.g. milliseconds, ~49 days in u32) and document it in the route's description; at minimum report saturation in the JSON so the page can say the record stopped.","summary":"Write stamps are u32 microseconds and saturate at about 71.6 minutes of board time, after which the memory view silently stops updating","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:56.121979Z","id":"e30ae6133a4e2bed62e33e4742118bf93e425c8b2cfb8a6cca26778207838405","body":{"detail":"Bus::write computes write_stamp(self.now) on every RAM store (bus.rs:342), a 64-bit division per store on the interpreter's hottest data path, and writes `written[word]` and `writer[word]` in two separate Vecs, i.e. two more cache lines touched per store. Ram::new allocates 8 bytes of record per 4 bytes of RAM (bus.rs:88-92), so the H7's 1060 KiB of RAM carries 2120 KiB of record in the container's linear memory, which docs/time-travel-debugger.md:281-291 describes checkpointing as page differences: a store now dirties up to three pages instead of one.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":332},"end":{"line":345},"content_hash":"663d333afd8f6cb287c5a44b0320cea9e7560a1cefbd883107f02183533a1120"},"suggested_fix":"Compute the stamp once where `bus.now` is assigned (keep a `stamp: u32` beside `now`, or set both through one setter) instead of on every store, and store the record as one Vec<[u32; 2]> (stamp, writer) so a store touches one extra cache line. Measure with the existing Hubris boot benchmark before and after.","summary":"Each RAM store now pays a u64 divide by 1,000,000 and two extra bounds-checked writes into separate arrays, and RAM's footprint triples","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:56.122008Z","id":"3833a20f01bce2aca16c35794190a421eababa0ad027008075aea9d8ddcdf424","body":{"detail":"All current RAM writers are marked (Bus::write at bus.rs:342-344, DMA via dma_wrote at bus.rs:366-370), but `pub fn ram(&mut self, base) -> Option<&mut Vec<u8>>` (bus.rs:203) still lets any caller change RAM without a mark, and `Ram::bytes` is pub (tests/thumb2.rs:92 writes it directly). Bus::write marks just `offset / 4` (bus.rs:341-343), which is right only because every caller aligns first (exec.rs:205 faults unaligned stores and exec.rs:342-353 splits ARMv7-M unaligned stores into byte stores, dap.rs:252 aligns TAR, Machine::debug_write writes aligned words or bytes); a future caller passing an unaligned halfword or word would change two words and mark one. Ram::mark (bus.rs:94) already handles spans.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":202},"end":{"line":205},"content_hash":"4963ed8293870034cb6d3d6714f541fcfa7eef3c6a6a6fd4b91e5442fb09b195"},"suggested_fix":"Remove Bus::ram or make it return `&[u8]`; use `ram.mark(offset, size as usize, ..)` in Bus::write (or debug_assert alignment there) so the record does not depend on every caller aligning.","summary":"The write record is kept by convention only: Bus::ram hands out an unmarked &mut Vec<u8>, Ram's fields are pub, and Bus::write marks only the word at offset/4","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/exception.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:56.122048Z","id":"83435593af1b49a249a7f3cdc765f0be235ecf3dc9c2935ccf83c009887c962f","body":{"detail":"The record only needs `bus.writer = BY_EXCEPTION`; the added `self.bus.now = self.time` (exception.rs:95) also changes what Bus::write passes to a peripheral when the frame address (a corrupt SP) decodes to one, since before this change bus.now still held the time of the last instruction's access. That is arguably more correct, but it is a change to execution bundled into a change meant only to observe it, and checkpoints or tapes recorded before it would not replay identically in that case.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":95},"end":{"line":96},"content_hash":"f2b600069ae52ef8c01695546c44f9a1360ed8def4567bbef7a2da3048877749"},"suggested_fix":"Either drop the bus.now assignment here and keep the commit observation-only, or keep it and say in the commit message that it changes stacking-into-peripheral timing.","summary":"Exception entry now sets bus.now before stacking, which changes the time a peripheral sees if a frame is stacked into peripheral space","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:56.122073Z","id":"ae10af9f23726265ac03a8b435e2fb81116065483be764d6dd79b02f6399eee9","body":{"detail":"Lines 363-364 (`len bytes of RAM at addr, if they are all in one RAM: what a DMA reaches.`) belong to dma_slice but now sit directly above dma_wrote's own `///` line, so they read as one doc block on dma_wrote. dma_wrote also repeats dma_slice's RAM lookup.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":363},"end":{"line":372},"content_hash":"cf6824b706c98847e6aab668fd9409e95e36e79fca4ba2ee373895ef13a309b0"},"suggested_fix":"Move dma_wrote below dma_slice (or above the dma_slice comment); optionally have dma_slice return the Ram and offset so the write and the mark share one lookup.","summary":"dma_wrote was inserted between dma_slice and its doc comment, so rustdoc gives dma_wrote both descriptions and dma_slice none","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:47:23.193180Z","id":"73e4d681566b34b2a495ca9cccad2f9ac6731671111e403a7b9cb821d34c3bd6","body":{"detail":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:225 and :246 test `last > since`. Machine::debug_write sets bus.now = self.time (crates/armv6m/src/lib.rs:672), so with board time frozen its stamp is write_stamp(time), the same value writes_json returns as `now` (crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1455). web/memory.js:336 stores `this.now = answer.now` and :309 polls with since = this.now, so the probe's write is never reported. The existing test crates/armv6m/tests/writes.rs:51 (`assert!(m.bus.written_since(stamp, 64).is_empty())`) encodes the exclusive boundary. Concrete user-visible failure: a probe write while halted never lights its cell.","kind":"comment","references":"1fcead51d80302234c7ea809a457b02f730ff49d624ecc77ea5d05f71250c985","summary":"confirmed: a write stamped equal to the `now` a poll returned is skipped by `last > since`","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:47:23.193211Z","id":"1a5adb963088e9ab05e6ab86b0a231179803ac5d28f14fcbc2c848c29a70dfa1","body":{"detail":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:241 computes `((base - ram.base) as usize + length as usize).div_ceil(4)`; on wasm32 (armv6m is a dependency of the kernel, crates/kernel/Cargo.toml:31; README.md:40,98) usize is 32-bit, so offset 0x100 + length 0xFFFFFFF0 wraps (no overflow-checks in release) to 0xF0, to = 0x3C < from = 0x40, and `ram.written[from..to]` at bus.rs:244 panics. Length reaches there unclamped from crates/kernel/src/server.rs:170-173 (number() accepts 0x hex, server.rs:666-670) via board.rs:1469. Also confirmed: server.rs:166,173 truncate with `as u32`, and board.rs:1472 echoes the unclamped length. Kind concern is right (malformed hand-typed path, not normal page use).","kind":"comment","references":"2338ac20970d673fa6e2aece284b71123d8efce100bb61bddd6cc5f72d29993e","summary":"confirmed: written_in adds offset + length in usize, which wraps on wasm32 and panics on the slice","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:47:23.193228Z","id":"8e975ea53fb0535f2593692d8c9bd2f1ddb336362cb423f8113863998d2eac5d","body":{"detail":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:83-85 `(now / 1_000_000).saturating_add(1).min(u32::MAX as u64) as u32`; the doc at bus.rs:67-68 admits it saturates at about 71 minutes. Once saturated, writes_json's `now` is u32::MAX, web/memory.js:309 polls with since = u32::MAX, and `last > since` (bus.rs:225, 246) is never true; nothing in the JSON reports saturation.","kind":"comment","references":"1cfb2c5edc394fe6d926f8d263e00f80d131c41f544df60682defa7a6e4c50b5","summary":"confirmed: stamps saturate at u32::MAX (~71.6 min of board time) and polls then never see another write","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:47:23.193235Z","id":"c80ff83bc4db9baef9a4efe263af8ba25366d10b181f04f7bc4c994bf7369bc5","body":{"detail":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:341-343 call write_stamp(self.now) (a u64 / 1_000_000, bus.rs:84) and write ram.written[word] and ram.writer[word] on every store; Ram::new (bus.rs:88-91) allocates written and writer as two Vec<u32> of size.div_ceil(4) each, 8 bytes of record per 4 of RAM. Suggestion kind fits: a cost, not a defect.","kind":"comment","references":"e30ae6133a4e2bed62e33e4742118bf93e425c8b2cfb8a6cca26778207838405","summary":"confirmed: every RAM store divides u64 by 1e6 and writes two separate record Vecs; record is 2x the RAM","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:47:23.193242Z","id":"af2285dbe3a7108e71180466d4aafb58f795cdca6c46e4f27ea09f925e9ab57b","body":{"detail":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:202-204 `pub fn ram(&mut self, base: u32) -> Option<&mut Vec<u8>>`; Ram's fields are pub (bus.rs:64-72); Bus::write marks `let word = offset / 4` only (bus.rs:341-343) even for a size-2/4 write, while Ram::mark (bus.rs:94-103) handles spans. Current callers align, so suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"3833a20f01bce2aca16c35794190a421eababa0ad027008075aea9d8ddcdf424","summary":"confirmed: Bus::ram hands out unmarked &mut Vec<u8> and Bus::write marks only offset/4","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/exception.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:47:23.193248Z","id":"eac14212d220ae9c4b22aabee8516a541bb23bb1b78073922bf38903e472686f","body":{"detail":"`git log -L95,96:crates/armv6m/src/exception.rs` shows both `self.bus.now = self.time;` and `self.bus.writer = BY_EXCEPTION;` added in 6fb0635 (the write-record commit). Stacking goes through self.bus.write (exception.rs:103), which passes self.now to soc.write for a frame address decoding to a peripheral (bus.rs:355-356). Before, bus.now was only set at instruction access (exec.rs:142, 213) etc. Suggestion kind fits.","kind":"comment","references":"83435593af1b49a249a7f3cdc765f0be235ecf3dc9c2935ccf83c009887c962f","summary":"confirmed: the recording commit added bus.now = self.time before stacking, changing the time stacking writes pass to peripherals","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:47:23.193254Z","id":"45a616e37fc76c45d15021259f19866c665fe7e24eed28b145ad6e23c232297e","body":{"detail":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:363-365: `/// len bytes of RAM at addr, if they are all in one RAM: what a DMA` / `/// reaches.` / `/// len bytes of RAM at addr written by DMA at now.` then `fn dma_wrote` at 366; `fn dma_slice` at 372 has no doc comment. dma_wrote (366-370) repeats dma_slice's lookup (372-376).","kind":"comment","references":"ae10af9f23726265ac03a8b435e2fb81116065483be764d6dd79b02f6399eee9","summary":"confirmed: dma_slice's doc comment now sits above dma_wrote, leaving dma_slice undocumented","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504147Z","id":"462058256010ab1842a61161b03d416aa21b81b9e178228c6a968b15742f84ed","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5f3e6d9","summary":"fixed: Mark.stamp is u64 and write_stamp no longer saturates (crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs); test stamps_do_not_saturate_on_a_long_run (two hours of board time stamps above u32::MAX and keep increasing) passes; the page holds stamps in Float64Array (web/memory.js picture(), git:c852755).","supersedes":"1cfb2c5edc394fe6d926f8d263e00f80d131c41f544df60682defa7a6e4c50b5","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504210Z","id":"96781fcb02e415962279b19ce696162b7dd0eed6c1f7736ca2e23bc42b9b4bd5","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5f3e6d9","summary":"fixed: Bus::ram (unused) removed; Bus::write marks through Ram::mark(offset, size) so a write spanning words marks each; Ram::marks documents that direct changes to bytes leave no mark. cargo test --release: all suites pass.","supersedes":"3833a20f01bce2aca16c35794190a421eababa0ad027008075aea9d8ddcdf424","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504237Z","id":"ca05641327174db099349c20be887b9f333901f945470cbde4d26c798710ea25","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5f3e6d9","summary":"fixed: written_since and written_in now report cells stamped at or after `since` (crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs written_since/written_in, `last >= since`). Tests crates/armv6m/tests/writes.rs cells_written_at_or_since_a_stamp_are_found_and_no_others and a_probe_write_while_the_board_stands_still_is_not_lost pass (cargo test --release --test writes: 9 passed).","supersedes":"1fcead51d80302234c7ea809a457b02f730ff49d624ecc77ea5d05f71250c985","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504258Z","id":"85769d79d48d0e786b431e23d836f31cf39978ea12834a964f32867e136c582e","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5f3e6d9","summary":"fixed: written_in computes the end in u64 and clamps it to the RAM (bus.rs written_in), returns None for a base outside RAM; server.rs parses address/length/cell with number32 (u32::try_from, refusing larger); writes_in_json clamps length to the RAM and answers no RAMs for a non-RAM base. Test part_of_a_ram_stops_at_its_end_however_long_it_is_asked_for (lengths u32::MAX-8 and u32::MAX) passes.","supersedes":"2338ac20970d673fa6e2aece284b71123d8efce100bb61bddd6cc5f72d29993e","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504280Z","id":"cad3cf8888ffb9b9b7edfd6e0027367ddb1420011b9504e4b921a27dee6e72fa","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5f3e6d9","summary":"fixed: the record is one Vec<Mark> (stamp and writer together, one extra cache line per store) and Bus::stamp() recomputes write_stamp only when bus.now changed. Measured: demo/hubris/check.mjs \"humility tasks: 15.0 s, 2736 M instructions\" after, against 15.1 s before.","supersedes":"e30ae6133a4e2bed62e33e4742118bf93e425c8b2cfb8a6cca26778207838405","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504301Z","id":"e7a29c1f376e25f74031ecb9d51eb35ce8f0f86cf7ff8d7b872797c952155d8c","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5f3e6d9","summary":"fixed: dma_slice keeps its own doc comment and dma_wrote follows it with its own (crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs).","supersedes":"ae10af9f23726265ac03a8b435e2fb81116065483be764d6dd79b02f6399eee9","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/exception.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504321Z","id":"e44fe7995d9046dd6151e05e63f1496d47ac168647b3070e3abad2b99659e773","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5f3e6d9","summary":"fixed: exception.rs no longer assigns bus.now on entry; only bus.writer = BY_EXCEPTION is set, with a comment that the frame is stamped at the last access's time. Test an_exception_s_frame_is_the_core_s passes; demo/hubris/replay.mjs: 12 of 12 board instants landed on exactly, 0 different from every checkpoint.","supersedes":"83435593af1b49a249a7f3cdc765f0be235ecf3dc9c2935ccf83c009887c962f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ltdc.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:03.272669Z","id":"9e6b51b467b91238fca04c6ef7a0869d12154b4a1ecaa6f3e20a827ffaf077ef","body":{"detail":"Nothing scans between slices, so the panel is read whole when asked; a complete model would time the active area and blanking from the pixel clock (PLL3R) and the timing registers, take VBR at the blank, raise the line and reload interrupts, and refuse to drive the panel without a pixel clock, which Hubris's Doom app does not configure.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":190},"end":{"line":196},"content_hash":"0341ff85d891b5cf3171abda8f46530faa4c72837ba802b7e2e45bd537efe03e"},"summary":"VBR reloads at once rather than at the next vertical blank","tags":["session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/quadspi.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:03.272722Z","id":"3249c5a50e62ea7d86a3b816f109b0d37c6a5d32b07e5718c6258bbba91e8aef","body":{"detail":"A driver that programs or erases the NOR (indirect write, status polling, the FIFO, DLR/AR/DR) gets registers that only remember what was written. A complete version would speak the flash's command set as devices::W25q80 does over SPI.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":99},"end":{"line":110},"content_hash":"158d861e492efe20326036ac8364a784d20216c9ea287b336f5301ecc79ece23"},"summary":"Only memory-mapped mode is modelled; indirect and polling modes do nothing","tags":["session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/timing.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:03.272748Z","id":"8574e9c6a148f80cb7dd80b04fb6f6aa30dc524f5add6ac376ca1fcf0ce4ef39","body":{"detail":"No cache misses are modelled, so with the M7's D-cache on every read of the NOR is free. A real H7 pays a line fill per 32 bytes missed through the QUADSPI at its clock; a complete model would charge misses.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":84},"end":{"line":84},"content_hash":"d884b3a6ddd13fa01cdc98644ec9351c542a95aedb676382897f334f181477e8"},"summary":"QUADSPI window cost is a guess: free when D-cache is on, 160 core cycles a read when off","tags":["session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:44.413679Z","id":"1302396899c9400bd7940efde9bcdbc41264b7831dd9acd5b9d74e41b287bfef","body":{"detail":"Failure: following everything, a loop `adds r1, #1; ldr r2, [r3, r1, lsl #2]` that has run more than about 4096 times in the window. Each `adds` makes a Merge step [prev, 0] (provenance.rs settle), so r1's history is a chain longer than 4096 nodes. Provenance::origins (provenance.rs:414-436) stops when `seen.len() > 4096` (or after `most` = 8 origins) and returns whatever it found so far, here nothing. only_pointers then evaluates `.all(..)` over an empty Vec, which is true. through() (provenance.rs:509) therefore treats the Load's address as saying nothing and replaces the Load with the loaded value, and derive() drops the Chose branch, so board/derive shows the table entry with nothing that chose it. The same misclassification happens when an address has more than 8 origins and the first 8 found happen to be pointers.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":617},"end":{"line":621},"content_hash":"b7897a2b8ae23b4ac637c99e84f19364656bd244ee2a6b242af0809c05b3d7c0"},"suggested_fix":"Make the walk report truncation (e.g. an origins variant returning (Vec, complete: bool)) and have only_pointers return false unless the walk completed and found at least one origin, or found only Register{13|15}. Add a test with a loop index stepped more than 4096 times.","summary":"only_pointers reads a truncated origins() list as 'all pointers', so the index of a lookup with a long history is dropped from the derivation","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:44.413749Z","id":"f93e1a3c36a74cc92bb6ceaa41c5b12ef5bc4e65c86803df2dda1b46f3731b16","body":{"detail":"Failure: `board/provenance on` (Machine::follow_values -> Provenance::new, lib.rs:580), with r1 = 0xff set before following began; the code then reads RXDR into r0 and runs `ands r0, r1`. settle (provenance.rs:308) picks r1 as the untagged register read and takes `self.values[1]`, which is the default 0 because only Provenance::everything initialises `values` (provenance.rs:166) and settle only updates written registers. The step node is Merge { constant: Some(0) }, and node_json (crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1848-1863), which falls back to `constant` when the instruction has no immediate, reports `\"imm\":0` in board/why. The forward mode (Provenance::seeded, lib.rs:497) has the same problem.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":160},"end":{"line":185},"content_hash":"e116b74e5ce5ef9532377a6cc9630673634ce4cdf898dace34d657a6fc199da0"},"suggested_fix":"Take the constant from the `values` argument settle already receives when the register was not also written by the instruction (values[n] is the live register value then), falling back to self.values only for a register the instruction both read and wrote; or pass the machine's registers to new/seeded and initialise `values` there.","summary":"Provenance::new and ::seeded leave `values` all zero, so a merge's `constant` reads 0 for any register not written since following began","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/exception.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:44.413784Z","id":"26320de88f6a7a29b065d3b32e4ccbff7622426ccc4a1301abf09d0385339230","body":{"detail":"Exception entry writes `self.cpu.r[14] = exc_return` and sets `provenance.regs[14] = 0`, but does not update `provenance.values[14]`. settle (provenance.rs:308) deliberately takes lr as a source of constants (`n != 13`), and an untagged lr is exactly what exception entry produces even when following everything. A handler instruction that merges lr with a followed value (e.g. `orr.w r0, r0, lr`) then reports as its constant whatever lr held before the exception, not EXC_RETURN. Exception return (exception.rs:323-324) was updated to keep `values` in step; entry was not.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":168},"end":{"line":171},"content_hash":"510a9f5a843cf7f276eb2ed404454aa4099ed1103ef6f1a7dfa02942eb690c97"},"suggested_fix":"Set `provenance.values[14] = exc_return` next to `provenance.regs[14] = 0` at exception.rs:170, or adopt the fix of reading untagged registers' values from settle's `values` argument so no write path has to remember to update the copy.","summary":"Exception entry sets lr to EXC_RETURN and clears its tag but leaves provenance.values[14] stale","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:44.413817Z","id":"cb3dff7a3a1ae9deebb313272fe10127cea42f619b6ca01f028fe42f86dffb3d","body":{"detail":"For each Store node in a derivation tree with repeats, the loop at provenance.rs:563 walks back one repeat at a time (`while let Some(before) = self.repeated(earliest)`, capped at `1 << 20`), and each repeated() (provenance.rs:648-669) allocates a VecDeque and visits up to 64 nodes. A counter or loop-carried sum stored a million times in the window costs ~64M node visits for one node of the tree, and a tree can hold several such stores; none of it is charged against Walk::budget (400 in crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1904), so a board/derive request can stall the board server. When the cap is hit, `earliest` is silently not the earliest and `repeats` is reported as exactly 1048576. Mark::run (bus.rs:84-89) was added in the same change to answer 'since when has this code stepped it' cheaply.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":555},"end":{"line":572},"content_hash":"52b7eaa85953dba58f0b1f93790813368bec3f4fe1f7a8dc9df64c167ae2cebe"},"suggested_fix":"Bound the fold by the walk's budget or by time, and mark the result as truncated (e.g. a `repeats_cut: bool` in Derived) when the cap is hit; or index stores by (addr, pc) as they are recorded so the earliest of a run is found without re-walking the chain.","summary":"derive() folds a count by calling repeated() up to 2^20 times, each a BFS of up to 64 nodes, outside the walk's budget","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:44.413849Z","id":"aafeb1d93ba8623ae9b502fcbdb359a3deefaab49df813348a2daf3f15ee468f","body":{"detail":"In the Armv7-M ARM (https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0403/latest/, A5.3.12 'Data-processing (register)'), op1=1xxx with op2=00xx is parallel addition/subtraction (signed) and op2=01xx (unsigned); the miscellaneous operations (QADD, QDADD, QSUB, QDSUB, REV, RBIT, SEL, CLZ) are op1=10xx with op2=10xx (A5.3.15). ops.rs:279 tests `op1 >> 2 == 0b10 && op2 >> 2 == 0b00` for the saturating ops. Checked with clang --target=thumbv7em: `sadd16 r0, r1, r2` = fa91 f002 gives op1=1001, op2=0000 and is named \"qadd\"; `qadd r0, r1, r2` = fa82 f081 gives op1=1000, op2=1000, misses that test, and in the misc arm (op1&3, op2&3) = (0, 0) is not listed, so it is named \"op\". board/derive shows this as the merge's `op` (crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1863).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":268},"end":{"line":296},"content_hash":"afd718ded192d92877fe6c0c7bb8bd8e0d243aa41356ad3e6769983172160e8e"},"suggested_fix":"Move the QADD/QDADD/QSUB/QDSUB case into the misc arm as `(0b00, n) => [\"qadd\", \"qdadd\", \"qsub\", \"qdsub\"][n]` and drop the op2 00xx test so parallel add/sub falls through to the add/sub fallback; add qadd (fa82 f081) and sadd16 (fa91 f002) to instructions_are_named_by_what_they_do_to_values.","summary":"data_register decodes QADD/QSUB in the wrong op2 range, naming SADD16 'qadd' and QADD 'op'","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:44.413872Z","id":"a3fd0c296790c2c0da5fc04565893fdc7ee251bdce8720742c2691c9c04f6c2d","body":{"detail":"For the 0101 group, bits 11:9 are opB: 000 STR, 001 STRH, 010 STRB, 011 LDRSB, 1xx loads (Armv7-M ARM A5.2.4, https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0403/latest/). ops.rs:127 takes bit 11 alone as the load bit, so `ldrsb r0, [r1, r2]` (0x5688, checked with clang) is named \"str\". The address merge of exactly this instruction, a signed byte looked up in a table by an index, is a Merge node (provenance.rs loaded sets value: Some(addr)), so board/derive shows it as op \"str\".","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":126},"end":{"line":132},"content_hash":"d3fa878fd6542897735fb19aeab23b9e10f9828386295f1456bb1b5488ca6344"},"suggested_fix":"For hw >> 12 == 0b0101 test `hw >> 9 & 7 >= 3` for a load; keep bit 11 for the immediate-offset groups.","summary":"narrow_operation names LDRSB (register offset) 'str'","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:44.413892Z","id":"c9de3c6cfe90a4285a4ee58fec3bb0a505e93402a713a2d481d812745bd03e78","body":{"detail":"A shift of type 3 with imm3:imm2 = 0 is RRX (Armv7-M ARM A7.4.2 DecodeImmShift), not ROR #0: `rrx r0, r1` = ea4f 0031 is named \"ror\" (ops.rs:174-184) with immediate Value(0) (ops.rs:82-87), and `add.w r0, r1, r2, rrx` = eb01 0032 gives shifted_operand Some((\"ror\", 0)), so the inspector would render `ror 0`, a no-op, for a rotate through carry. Separately, ops.rs:187-192 names everything in the 1110 100x x1xx group ldrd/strd, which includes LDREX (e851 0f00), STREX and TBB/TBH.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":40},"end":{"line":48},"content_hash":"ec7368b03b3087285866f6b88ee9f8020d973f0ef17a60c568dadffa1ecadd1a"},"suggested_fix":"Return (\"rrx\", 1) / \"rrx\" for (3, 0); distinguish LDREX/STREX and TBB/TBH within the dual/exclusive group, or name the group \"op\".","summary":"RRX is reported as 'ror' by 0 in shifted_operand, immediate and wide_operation; LDREX/STREX/TBB as ldrd/strd","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/dwarf.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:44.413941Z","id":"232950fd4eed40cb2cf16992a51c25b5c1d368b04595d9a6390d80ca4d897829","body":{"detail":"DWARF 5 section 6.2.5.1 defines line 0 as code not attributable to any source line; compilers use it for code merged from several lines and for branch landings. Debug::lines steps back 2 bytes at a time (up to 64) and copies the first nonzero line in the same function, i.e. the textually preceding instruction, which at a branch target is typically the end of the other arm or the loop body, not where control came from. The result replaces out[0] with no marker, so board/source and the derivation's source lines present a guess as fact. `frames` also returns line 0 when there is no location at all, so code without line info pays up to 32 extra find_frames lookups per call.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":299},"end":{"line":319},"content_hash":"8dbb12d3b0f58961b50b80b436738b23a23239434ca9e34751a2fad052e3732f"},"suggested_fix":"Add a flag to Line (e.g. `inferred: bool`) set when the line was borrowed so the UI can show it as approximate, and skip the walk when the frame has no location at all rather than an explicit line 0.","summary":"Line-0 fallback borrows the line of the preceding address, not of the code that reaches it, and the caller cannot tell it was borrowed","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:13:24.641964Z","id":"ce09d8a5b050715163fba09f085fa4cd7c17ec62c8737a170512fd20f7fe3ba1","body":{"detail":"provenance.rs:419 breaks the origins BFS at seen.len() > 4096 and returns what it has; only_pointers (provenance.rs:617-619) applies .all(says_nothing) to that partial list. Reproduced with a throwaway test (since removed) under crates/armv6m/tests: `ldr r3, =0x24000100; movs r1,#0; ldr r2,=N; begin: 1: adds r1,#1; subs r2,#1; bne 1b; ands r1,r1,#3; ldrb r6,[r3,r1]; strb r6,[r7]`, follow_everything_from(begin), derivation(memory[0x24000200], 40, 400, says_nothing as in tests/provenance.rs plus Register{13|15}). N=10: load operands [Value Held@0x24000102, Chose Merge{value:0x24000102}]. N=5000: top operand is the Held entry directly, no Load, no Chose anywhere in the tree. Here the partial list was [Register r3 = 0x24000100], a pointer, so all() was true; an empty list gives true as well.","kind":"comment","references":"1302396899c9400bd7940efde9bcdbc41264b7831dd9acd5b9d74e41b287bfef","summary":"confirmed: a lookup whose index has >4096 steps of history loses its Chose branch","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:13:24.642012Z","id":"33250d2deb88930808fb898f73d1f3beff716d39dcf3c4b54b7e8153f88645ca","body":{"detail":"Provenance::new (provenance.rs:160-162) and ::seeded (175-183) leave values = [0;16]; only everything() (166) initialises it, settle (provenance.rs:353-357) updates only written registers, and the constant is read from self.values (provenance.rs:308). Throwaway test (since removed): H753, `movs r1,#0xff` run for one instruction, then m.follow_values(true), then read I2C2 RXDR into r4 and `adds r4, r1`: p.regs[4] node = Merge { value: Some(267), constant: Some(0) } while cpu.r[1] = 0xff. crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1857-1862 emits that constant as \"imm\" when ops::immediate is None, which it is for narrow `adds r4, r1` (ops.rs:72 needs bit 10 set).","kind":"comment","references":"f93e1a3c36a74cc92bb6ceaa41c5b12ef5bc4e65c86803df2dda1b46f3731b16","summary":"confirmed: under follow_values a merge's constant is 0 for a register set before following began","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/exception.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:13:24.642025Z","id":"fa1b7bcbb2d48053d2f0d04624b59dc33238ab01c1bb3173deecbb8ba6dbfef6","body":{"detail":"exception.rs:155 sets cpu.r[14] = exc_return and exception.rs:170 sets provenance.regs[14] = 0, with no write to provenance.values[14]; the only values[] write outside provenance.rs is exception.rs:324 on the return path (grep -rn '\\.values\\[' crates/armv6m/src crates/kernel/src). settle takes the constant from self.values[n] for an untagged lr (provenance.rs:308; n != 13 excludes only sp). Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"26320de88f6a7a29b065d3b32e4ccbff7622426ccc4a1301abf09d0385339230","summary":"confirmed: exception entry leaves provenance.values[14] stale","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:13:24.642037Z","id":"6c73afc26f27c817b74ba99c919cd448e577f58a4ea8496744745539fccaecfa","body":{"detail":"provenance.rs:562-571 calls repeated() up to `1 << 20` times with no walk.budget charge and no truncation marker; repeated() (provenance.rs:648-669) allocates a VecDeque and visits up to 64 nodes per call. Throwaway test (since removed): a counter at 0x24000180 incremented 1,200,000 times under follow_everything_from; derivation(..., 40, 400, ..) returned repeats = 1048576 (the cap, not 1.2M) in 36 ms (release build). For this simple counter each repeated() visits about 3 nodes, so the 64M-visit worst case needs a longer chain per step, but the uncharged cost and wrong count stand. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"cb3dff7a3a1ae9deebb313272fe10127cea42f619b6ca01f028fe42f86dffb3d","summary":"confirmed: the repeat fold is not charged to the budget and silently caps at 2^20","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:13:24.642048Z","id":"b157177fae9c0dc6851cf286401dcd729b42cfcab01e1ed21c926064ce1d9f44","body":{"detail":"clang --target=thumbv7em-none-eabihf -mcpu=cortex-m7 then llvm-objdump: `qadd r0,r1,r2` = fa82 f081, `sadd16 r0,r1,r2` = fa91 f002. wide_operation routes both to data_register (ops.rs:244). sadd16: op1=9, op2=0, so ops.rs:279 `op1>>2==0b10 && op2>>2==0b00` gives \"qadd\". qadd: op1=8, op2=8, reaching the misc arm (ops.rs:282-291) where (op1&3, op2&3) = (0,0) is not listed, giving \"op\". The Armv7-M ARM (https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0403/latest/) miscellaneous operations table places QADD/QDADD/QSUB/QDSUB at op1=00, op2=00..11 inside op1=10xx/op2=10xx; op2=00xx/01xx are parallel add/sub.","kind":"comment","references":"aafeb1d93ba8623ae9b502fcbdb359a3deefaab49df813348a2daf3f15ee468f","summary":"confirmed: sadd16 is named qadd and qadd is named op","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:13:24.642062Z","id":"c27f69f3a5e0b8b25a221f7de31447f465d190bff76402bb5a7ec7f4599b9cb5","body":{"detail":"clang then llvm-objdump: `ldrsb r0, [r1, r2]` = 5688. ops.rs:126-132: hw>>10 = 0b010101 falls in 0b010100..=0b100111 and bit 11 of 0x5688 is 0, so \"str\". In the Armv7-M ARM load/store single data item table (https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0403/latest/), opB = bits 11:9 = 011 is LDRSB (register).","kind":"comment","references":"a3fd0c296790c2c0da5fc04565893fdc7ee251bdce8720742c2691c9c04f6c2d","summary":"confirmed: ldrsb register-offset (0x5688) is named str","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:13:24.642070Z","id":"9567ab7cef0f09759b381baa636f48d720f3d3ddb46c6e9fd3ada36ddd24953b","body":{"detail":"clang then llvm-objdump: rrx r0,r1 = ea4f 0031; add.w r0,r1,r2,rrx = eb01 0032; ldrex r0,[r1] = e851 0f00; tbb [r0,r1] = e8d0 f001; strex r2,r0,[r1] = e841 0200. shifted_operand: type 3, amount 0 hits ops.rs:47 `(kind, n)` giving (\"ror\", 0). wide_operation's move arm (ops.rs:173-183): shift 0 with type 3 gives SHIFTS[3] = \"ror\"; immediate gives Value(0) (ops.rs:87). ops.rs:187-193: e851 and e8d0 have bit6=1, bit4=1, giving \"ldrd\"; e841 has bit4=0, giving \"strd\". DecodeImmShift type 0b11 with imm5 = 0 is RRX (Armv7-M ARM, https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0403/latest/). Suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"c9de3c6cfe90a4285a4ee58fec3bb0a505e93402a713a2d481d812745bd03e78","summary":"confirmed: RRX reads as ror 0, and LDREX/STREX/TBB as ldrd/strd","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/dwarf.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:13:24.642080Z","id":"dfe384784588cd0c5dccc5bb8ee75969cbde570e8dafc7733eea7d88ce89d6a9","body":{"detail":"dwarf.rs:305-316: when out[0].line == 0 it steps back 2..=64 by 2 and overwrites out[0] with the first nonzero line in the same function; Line (dwarf.rs:121-126) has no field to mark it inferred. frames maps a missing location to line 0 (dwarf.rs:329 `unwrap_or(0)`), so a frame with no location also takes the up-to-32-lookup walk. Suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"232950fd4eed40cb2cf16992a51c25b5c1d368b04595d9a6390d80ca4d897829","summary":"confirmed: line-0 fallback copies the preceding address's line with no marker","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:18:30.853994Z","id":"5193327c3c79831706d001b8f472fe18eb32b1968c788c32abc0e9b662766822","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:bf13635","summary":"Fixed in bf13635: only_pointers uses origins_whole, which says whether every origin was found; a walk cut short is not taken to say nothing. Test an_index_stepped_longer_than_the_walk_looks_still_chose_its_entry (fails with the old rule).","supersedes":"1302396899c9400bd7940efde9bcdbc41264b7831dd9acd5b9d74e41b287bfef","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:18:30.893115Z","id":"658685f7b4a2333ad03a166f4652255d8ad591a7c93763099907a06743189591","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:bf13635","summary":"Fixed in bf13635: settle takes an untagged register's value from its live `values` unless the instruction wrote it; follow_values and seeded initialise Provenance::values from the registers. Test a_constant_followed_only_off_the_bus_is_what_its_register_held.","supersedes":"f93e1a3c36a74cc92bb6ceaa41c5b12ef5bc4e65c86803df2dda1b46f3731b16","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/exception.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:18:30.930287Z","id":"dca2bcda2a928f941bd18262f036ddf7d86899f94aff3e70f8fdd820f074f12f","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:bf13635","summary":"Fixed in bf13635: exception entry sets provenance.values[14] = exc_return beside regs[14] = 0.","supersedes":"26320de88f6a7a29b065d3b32e4ccbff7622426ccc4a1301abf09d0385339230","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/provenance.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:18:30.969173Z","id":"76bad67a438fdd9a73cda6f581fc6d4526b9cda3f74dd6de04056cf94fb272ce","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:bf13635","summary":"Fixed in bf13635: a node folds at most REPEATS (4096) repeats and says so (Derived::repeats_cut, served as repeats_cut, shown ×N+); counts longer than that are found by Mark::run on the page.","supersedes":"cb3dff7a3a1ae9deebb313272fe10127cea42f619b6ca01f028fe42f86dffb3d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:18:31.008887Z","id":"71dedf0746ddd132de3bb7f95cd2f481347d24ce4c36c197ff8ec020e6dea7a8","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:bf13635","summary":"Fixed in bf13635: QADD/QDADD/QSUB/QDSUB named in the op1 10xx op2 10xx arm; parallel add/sub fall through to add/sub. qadd and sadd16/ssub16 in the naming test.","supersedes":"aafeb1d93ba8623ae9b502fcbdb359a3deefaab49df813348a2daf3f15ee468f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:18:31.053128Z","id":"02400cbb2d22b082bd7ec8a4dfa18432315b414bff3503cab5daeaa3ffd19e23","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:bf13635","summary":"Fixed in bf13635: the 0101 register-offset group takes opB (bits 11:9) >= 3 as a load; ldrsb/strb/ldrh/strh in the naming test.","supersedes":"a3fd0c296790c2c0da5fc04565893fdc7ee251bdce8720742c2691c9c04f6c2d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:18:31.097047Z","id":"1a5eaef87dcc4e58f8cbd78346c8c44343ed4d0f09aa0f1300d031863d025e7d","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:bf13635","summary":"Fixed in bf13635: ROR #0 is RRX — shifted_operand gives ('rrx', 1), immediate none, move named rrx; LDREX/STREX (and byte/half/dual) and TBB/TBH named within the dual/exclusive group. Tests added.","supersedes":"c9de3c6cfe90a4285a4ee58fec3bb0a505e93402a713a2d481d812745bd03e78","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/armv6m/src/dwarf.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:18:31.142535Z","id":"9ed110aa75863c7c0bd427a5c050b6a1743bbce06ddef2ecb9a201bcfadf1225","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:bf13635","summary":"Fixed in bf13635: a borrowed line is Line::inferred, served as inferred:true and shown ≈ with a title saying it is likely; no walk where the frame has no line table at all.","supersedes":"232950fd4eed40cb2cf16992a51c25b5c1d368b04595d9a6390d80ca4d897829","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
